  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Vite Fallbacks**\n\nI\'m currently looking into Vite middleware with Express. I need to determine if a wildcard fallback is required in `server.ts` or during development through index.html, especially considering potential differences between Express v4 and v5. Specifically, I\'m trying to decide if I need to check for `*`, `*all`, `vite.transformIndexHtml`, or `dist`. I\'m starting to get closer to the solution.\n\n\n**Refining Fallback Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on potential gaps in fallback strategies. I see that various scenarios could cause issues, such as trailing slashes, subpaths, failed `index.html` serving, or Vite middleware missing non-root requests. I\'m also examining the need for wildcard routes (`*`) in both development and production. I\'m exploring if `app.use(\'*\', ...)` or `app.get(\'*\', ...)` might be necessary in development if `vite.middlewares` doesn\'t cover all HTML requests.\n\n\n**Confirming Middleware Behavior**\n\nI\'ve been verifying Vite middleware behavior. I\'ve confirmed that in `middlewareMode: true`, `vite.middlewares` handles assets, but crucially, it doesn\'t automatically serve `index.html` for root or subroutes. Without a fallback in Express, requests for `/` or subpaths will bypass Vite and result in a 404 from Express. I\'ve concluded this is the root cause.\n\n\n**Verifying Development Handling**\n\nI\'m now thoroughly examining how development mode functions with Vite middleware. I\'ve confirmed that `vite.middlewares` alone *won\'t* intercept root (`/`) requests. I\'m building out a test for the `server.ts` file, and I\'m adding `app.get(\'*\', ...)` to serve `index.html` transformed by Vite for all routes. My goal is to ensure a reliable fallback strategy.\n\n\n"
